#!/bin/sh
# .githooks/pre-commit - barrier: a staged plan (its DOOR `PLAN.md` /
# `PLAN_COURANT.md`, or an ARC of its `plan/` directory) judged ILLISIBLE by
# system/lib/mnemosyne/production/plan_format.py blocks the commit. Nothing else: this hook
# touches no other file.
#
# A PLAN IS A DIRECTORY (D166, clause 7): before YYYY-MM-DD the filter below did not
# Deployment-specific measurement omitted; the behavior is covered by tests.
# of a directory (`docs/plan/arc-c.md`, `projects/x/y/plan/arc-c.md`) matched it, `plans`
# stayed empty, `exit 0` - the barrier vanished WITHOUT A WORD as soon as the plan became a
# directory. This is the arc's first gesture, before any byte is moved: it is the safety net.
#
# A NESTED PLAN WAS JUDGED BY NOTHING (hole found by the third isolated audit, closed on
# YYYY-MM-DD) - and it is the SAME fault as the diff-status filter, one notch below.
# Real commit reproduced in a clone: `docs/gov/PLAN.md` + `docs/gov/plan/orphelin.md` never
# indexed -> `exit 0`, HEAD moves. The reader (`owning_plan`) does however implement clause 3
# of D166 correctly, « every directory carrying a door is a plan, AT EVERY LEVEL » ; it is the `case`
# below that knew only TWO hard-coded locations. A list of locations always forgets
# one more location, exactly as a list of statuses forgets one more status
# - and nesting is not a special case here, it is THE SHAPE being aimed at (a hundred or so
# theories, each with its strategies, ~150x the current volume).
# The prefilter therefore no longer asks ANY question of PLACE: it asks a question of SHAPE, and it
# is a SUPERSET of what `owning_plan` can claim - which claims only two things,
# (a) a file whose NAME is in `DOOR_FILENAMES`, (b) a path under `<dossier>/plan/`.
# (DEPTH already passed for what the prefilter saw: the `*` of an sh `case` traverses
# `/`, so `projects/trading/sample_lab/theories/T001/PLAN.md` was already judged - but no
# depth saved a plan placed anywhere other than under `docs/` or `projects/<bulle>/<projet>/`.)
#
# ERASING IS A PATH IN (hole found by isolated audit C20-5, closed on YYYY-MM-DD). The filter
# knew only `ACM`, which EXCLUDES `D` : `git rm` of an indexed arc put NOTHING into `plans`,
# `exit 0` in silence, and the door went on citing a vanished file - the refusal
# `index_sans_fichier` was never evaluated. Three rules close the CLASS, not the case:
#   1. `D` enters the filter, and the list of DELETED paths travels down to the judge;
#   2. `folder_form` stays true as soon as a staged DELETED path lives under `<dossier>/plan/`.
#      Without that the hole REOPENED on the gravest case: erasing the LAST arc empties
#      `<dossier>/plan/` from the index, `folder_form` falls back to false, and `index_sans_fichier` is
#      suppressed by the very clause that exists for single-file plans;
#   3. a DELETED path that no door claims any more is refused ONLY if it is a DOOR and
#      indexed arcs remain behind it. Erasing an ENTIRE plan (door + arcs, same
#      commit) passes: nothing is left that could lie, and refusing there would be pure friction.
#      Erasing the ONLY door while leaving the arcs is refused - an arc without a door belongs to
#      no plan. An ADDED or MODIFIED path that nothing claims stays refused as before.
#
# RENAMING TOO (hole found on YYYY-MM-DD) - and the general LESSON, which is worth more than the case.
# `git mv docs/plan/arc-c.md docs/plan/arc-d.md` produced `exit 0` and a HEAD that moves, the door
# now citing a file that no longer exists, whereas the SAME final state reached by
# `git rm` + `git add` was refused. Same state, two gestures, two verdicts: the defect was not
# a missing `D` and then a missing `R`, it was REASONING BY DIFF STATUS. A status filter
# always forgets one more status (`R`, `C`, `T`, and the one git will add). The barrier therefore
# now asks ONE question of STATE - « what is the RESULTING state of each touched plan? » - and
# no question of gesture: every staged path travels down (no `--diff-filter`), a
# rename is undone into a deletion + an addition (`--no-renames`) so that BOTH paths are
# judged, and « erased » is read in the RESULTING INDEX (`git ls-files`), not in a status.
#
# THE BARRIER NO LONGER COUNTS THE ARCS ITSELF (YYYY-MM-DD). `judge()` redid a plan's fold
# inline - `read_plan` + `plan_entries` + `merge_plan` + `index_problems` - that is a
# THIRD enumeration, while `plan_new_run.render()` printed to Operator « it is the same code
# Deployment-specific measurement omitted; the behavior is covered by tests.
# broken symlink under `plan/` (registry ILLISIBLE, barrier `exit 0`) and an empty `plan/` directory
# on the disk (likewise). It now goes through `fold_plan`, the single enumeration, the one
# `make plans`, `make ui-json` and `make plan-nouveau` already take - and at the same stroke it inherits
# the two PER-ARC refusals the copy did not have: `bloc_non_ferme` and `arc_vide`.
# The symlink is closed on BOTH sides (mode `120000` read from `git ls-files -s`, a single
# call to git as before). The EMPTY `plan/` directory CANNOT be - git does not track
# empty directories: the divergence is DECLARED in `judge()` and FROZEN by a test.
#
# Deployment-specific measurement omitted; the behavior is covered by tests.
# "illisible" on stdout without ever exiting non-zero (`python -c "print(...)"` ALWAYS
# exits 0, whatever it prints) - so the `&&` that chained it to the commit let it through.
# A check that PRINTS without EXITING NON-ZERO is not a barrier, it is a
# comment. This hook's rule: it exits 1 if (and only if) a staged plan is
# illisible, 0 otherwise - never an exit 0 accompanied by a failure message.
#
# core.hooksPath already points at this directory on this clone (Makefile:242-244, target `hooks`):
# this file is active as soon as it is present, with no other gesture (`make hooks` is still needed
# on a clone that has never run it).
#
# Emergency: `git commit --no-verify` short-circuits this hook (and every other one).

set -eu

repo_root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
cd "$repo_root"

# --- 1. a door faster than the wall --------------------------------------------------------
# This hook runs on EVERY commit of the repo: its empty-case cost must stay nil (a door slower
# than the wall is never used). ONE SINGLE call to git before the empty-case `exit 0`, and it is
# this one - all the rest (the judge, reading the index) happens only afterwards.
#
# NO `--diff-filter`, and `--no-renames` : the two halves of a single rule. What we want to
# know is not what HAPPENED to a path but which paths are staged, all of them, so as to
# go and read the STATE that results from it. With no filter, `A` `C` `D` `M` `T` `R` `U` all travel down, and
# so does the status git will add next year. `--no-renames` turns the pairing off: git
# no longer invents an `R`, a rename comes back as a deletion PLUS an addition, and BOTH
# paths are judged - the old one, which the door may still cite, and the new one, which the door
# may not know about. It also turns similarity detection off, so it costs LESS than
# the default.
#
# WHAT WAS WRITTEN HERE WAS FALSE, and a false comment is worse than no comment: it
# closes the question. It announced « a pure rename (status R, only if `diff.renames` is
# active - which is not the default here, verified on YYYY-MM-DD) ». `diff.renames` is TRUE
# Deployment-specific measurement omitted; the behavior is covered by tests.
# on YYYY-MM-DD (git 2.53.0) after `git mv docs/plan/arc-c.md docs/plan/arc-d.md`:
#     git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACMD  ->  (EMPTY)
#     git diff --cached --name-only                     ->  docs/plan/arc-d.md  (destination ONLY)
#     git diff --cached --name-status                   ->  R100  arc-c.md  arc-d.md
#     git diff --cached --name-only --no-renames        ->  arc-c.md AND arc-d.md
# Four commits wrongly passed: renaming an arc; renaming the DOOR (it evaporates, the arcs
# are left orphaned); renaming any file INTO `plan/` (arc never indexed); moving
# an arc from one plan to ANOTHER (both doors lie).
#
# `-z` IS NOT A DETAIL, it is a barrier that disappears. Without it, git QUOTES every path
# outside pure ASCII: an accented `docs/plan/arc oublie.md` comes back as
# `"docs/plan/arc oubli\303\251.md"`, quote included. The leading quote matches NO
# pattern of the `case` below, so the file is never judged and the hook exits 0 - in silence.
# Paths can contain accents. Validate this hook against synthetic non-ASCII paths rather than
# assuming plan paths are ASCII-only.
staged="$(git diff --cached --name-only --no-renames -z | tr '\0' '\n')"

# Isolate each path without breaking it on a space (for example
# "projects/sample/Example Project/PLAN.md"): IFS varies only for the duration of this split, on the
# newline alone (`git diff --name-only` yields one path per line, never quotes here).
old_ifs=$IFS
IFS='
'
set -- $staged
IFS=$old_ifs

# The two shapes of a plan, WITH NO PLACE AT ALL, and nothing else:
#   - the DOOR            a file NAMED `PLAN.md` / `PLAN_COURANT.md`, at any
#                         depth (`PLAN.md` at the root, `*/PLAN.md` everywhere else);
#   - an ARC of the dir   any `.md` under a `plan/` path segment, at any
#                         depth (`plan/*.md`, `*/plan/*.md`).
# `*` traverses `/`, so `docs/plan/c/arc-1.md`, `docs/gov/plan/orphelin.md` and
# `docs/a/plan/b/plan/c/PLAN.md` (three levels) all enter. The FALSE NEIGHBOURS stay out
# because BOTH edges of the `plan` segment are literal `/` in the pattern, and that is tested
# on a NAMED list: `docs/PLAN_ANCIEN.md` (the name is not the imposed one),
# `docs/plans/x.md` (plural directory), `docs/replan/x.md` (left edge),
# `docs/plan.md` (not a directory), `docs/plan/notes.txt` (not an arc).
#
# THESE FOUR PATTERNS ARE A COPY OF `DOOR_FILENAMES` / `ARCS_DIRNAME`, and a copy drifts.
# `tests/platform/test_pre_commit_plan_gate.py` re-reads THIS `case` and requires the set of its
# patterns to be exactly the one DERIVED from the Python constants - adding a third door name
# on the reader side without placing it here turns red, instead of silently making a plan invisible.
plans=""
for f in "$@"; do
    case "$f" in
        PLAN.md|PLAN_COURANT.md|*/PLAN.md|*/PLAN_COURANT.md)
            plans="${plans}${f}
"
            ;;
        plan/*.md|*/plan/*.md)
            plans="${plans}${f}
"
            ;;
    esac
done

if [ -z "$plans" ]; then
    exit 0
fi

# --- 2. judge EACH staged plan, on its INDEXED content, never the disk ---------------------
# TRAP AVOIDED: `git diff --cached` says WHAT is staged, but the content to judge is
# that of the INDEX - not of the file on disk, which can diverge (partial staging). Judging the
# working tree would bless a commit that was never read. `git show :<path>` reads exactly
# the indexed blob; that is what `read_plan` (system/lib/mnemosyne/production/plan_format.py,
# NOT modified by this hook - only imported) receives below.
export MNEMO_STAGED_PLANS="$plans"
# ALL staged paths, not only the plan-shaped ones: the judge works out for itself
# which ones have vanished, and it asks that question of `<dossier>/plan/`, not of a file name -
# a `docs/plan/notes.txt` leaving the index says « this plan WAS a directory » just as loudly as an
# arc. It is the same string as above: zero extra calls to git.
export MNEMO_STAGED_PATHS="$staged"
PYTHONPATH=system/lib uv run python - <<'PYEOF'
import os
import subprocess
import sys

from mnemosyne.production.plan_format import (
    ARCS_DIRNAME,
    DOOR_FILENAMES,
    ROLE_PLAN,
    PlanProblem,
    fold_plan,
    is_readable,
    owning_plan,
    unreadable_reasons,
)
from mnemosyne.production.plan_registry import ARC_ILLISIBLE, LIEN_SYMBOLIQUE

ETAT_ILLISIBLE = "illisible"

#: The git INDEX mode of a symbolic link, and the ONLY way this barrier can learn about one:
#: `git show :<path>` on a link hands back the TARGET PATH as if it were the file's own text, which
#: parses as a perfectly ordinary arc carrying no item and refuses nothing.
#:
#: [important] A SYMLINK IS TWO DIFFERENT FILES AT ONCE -- the index holds the LINK, the disk holds its
#: TARGET -- so this barrier (which reads the index, on purpose, and must) and `make plans` (which
# Deployment-specific measurement omitted; the behavior is covered by tests.
#: a broken link under `plan/` gave ILLISIBLE at the registry and `exit 0` here, and a valid one
#: would have diverged the other way. `plan_registry._read_text` refuses every symlink on the disk
#: side; refusing them here is what makes the four surfaces answer the same thing.
#:
# Deployment-specific measurement omitted; the behavior is covered by tests.
#: (3 in `.venv/bin`, all python interpreters), ZERO tracked by git. This refuses a shape nobody
#: writes and closes a divergence that was real.
SYMLINK_MODE = "120000"

#: WARNING: `LIEN_SYMBOLIQUE` IS IMPORTED, NEVER RETYPED. A person must meet ONE sentence whether they
#: hit this at `make plans` or at the barrier, and two expressions of one sentence drift -- which is
#: the very class this pass closes one level up. `plan_registry` owns it; here it is read.
#:
#: `plan_registry` also owns `LIEN_CASSE`, and this hook can never say it: on the DISK a link either
#: resolves or does not, while in the git INDEX a link has no resolvable target at all -- a mode
#: `120000` entry is simply a LINK. So the barrier says `LIEN_SYMBOLIQUE` for both, always, and it
#: is not an approximation but the only thing the index knows. Both are refused on both sides, so
#: the VERDICT agrees; the disk's extra precision about the cause is not a fact git holds.

#: `{chemin: mode}` for the RESULTING index. Filled by `indexed_tree()` below, which is called
#: unconditionally -- and which exits 1 rather than returning empty if git cannot answer, because an
#: empty map here would silently turn every symlink back into an ordinary file.
MODES = {}


def index_text(path):
    """The STAGED bytes of `path` (index, stage 0) -- never the working-tree copy on disk."""
    proc = subprocess.run(["git", "show", ":" + path], capture_output=True)
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        reason = proc.stderr.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip()
        return None, "lecture de l'index impossible (" + reason + ")"
    try:
        return proc.stdout.decode("utf-8"), ""
    except UnicodeDecodeError as exc:
        return None, "encodage invalide dans l'index (%s)" % exc


def read_indexed(path):
    """What the INDEX says `path` holds -- `(text, "")`, or `(None, why)`. `fold_plan`'s `read`.

    The mode is answered BEFORE the bytes, and the order is the rule: `git show` on a link succeeds
    and returns its target path, so asking the bytes first means never asking the mode at all.
    """
    if MODES.get(path) == SYMLINK_MODE:
        return None, LIEN_SYMBOLIQUE
    return index_text(path)


def lost(member, why):
    """One arc of THIS plan that could not be read -- and the rest of the plan is STILL judged.

    WARNING: It RETURNS a problem; it never returns from `judge`. The loop this replaces bailed out on the
    FIRST unreadable arc, which is exactly the blast radius D170 shrank on the registry side (ONE bad
    file out of thirteen took `scan_plans` from 43 items to 0, and every surface reading it went
    blank together). A lost arc now degrades ITSELF: the plan keeps its door and its readable arcs,
    and `is_readable` refuses ANY reading carrying a problem -- so the verdict stays ILLISIBLE and
    nothing that used to be refused is waved through. Only the blast radius shrinks.

    `line=0` and no `source`: the shape `arc_hors_index` and `arc_vide` already use for a refusal
    about a FILE rather than a line. The path travels in the detail.
    """
    return PlanProblem(
        0,
        ARC_ILLISIBLE,
        "%s : %s - ses items MANQUENT au plan jugé ; répare ce fichier et remets-le en scène"
        % (member, why or "introuvable"),
    )


def indexed_tree():
    """Every path the INDEX holds, WITH its mode -- `(chemins, {chemin: mode})`.

    `-s` rather than a second call to git, and that is the entire cost of the symlink rule: one
    `git ls-files` was already being run for `tree`, and `-s` puts the mode in the very same bytes.
    This hook runs on EVERY commit of the repo, so a door slower than the wall is never used.

    `-z` so a space, colon, or non-ASCII character in a name never becomes two paths or a quoted
    representation. A quoted path matches no arc or index entry.

    Each record is `MODE SP SHA SP STAGE TAB chemin`, split on the FIRST tab so a tab inside a file
    name stays part of the name. The keys come back DE-DUPLICATED and in index order: `-s` lists an
    unmerged path once per stage, and two identical arcs would collide on their own identities.
    """
    proc = subprocess.run(["git", "ls-files", "-s", "-z"], capture_output=True)
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        # FAIL-CLOSED, and this is not decorative prudence: this return was `()` for as long as
        # the list of erased paths came from its own `git diff`. Now that « erased » MEANS
        # « absent from this tree », an empty tree reads as « everything has been erased » - and every
        # erased path that no door claims any more is ignored by construction. A mute `ls-files`
        # would therefore have switched the ENTIRE barrier off by returning 0. We exit 1 and say so.
        sys.stderr.write(
            "[pre-commit] `git ls-files` a echoue -- l'index resultant est illisible, donc aucun "
            "plan ne peut etre juge. Commit refuse.\n"
        )
        sys.exit(1)
    modes = {}
    for record in proc.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\0"):
        if not record:
            continue
        head, _, path = record.partition("\t")
        modes[path] = head.split(" ", 1)[0]
    return tuple(modes), modes


def folder_of(path):
    """The directory part of a `/`-joined path -- `""` at the root, never the file's own name."""
    return path.rsplit("/", 1)[0] if "/" in path else ""


def arcs_left_behind(door_path, tree):
    """Does `<folder>/plan/` still hold a markdown arc in the INDEX, once `door_path` is erased?

    The whole tie-break of the ERASE class. Deleting a plan ENTIRELY (door and arcs in the same
    commit) leaves nothing that can lie, so it must pass; deleting only the door leaves arcs that
    belong to no plan and are judged by nothing, so it must be refused. The question is asked of the
    INDEX, which is already the post-commit state -- an arc deleted in this same commit is gone
    from it, which is exactly why the two cases separate here and nowhere else.
    """
    folder = folder_of(door_path)
    under = (folder + "/" if folder else "") + ARCS_DIRNAME + "/"
    return any(p.startswith(under) and p.endswith(".md") for p in tree)


def judge(folder, tree, deleted=()):
    """Read the WHOLE plan rooted at `folder` from the index, and say why it is refused (or not).

    The unit judged is the PLAN, never the staged file: an arc alone has no cap and would be
    refused by a rule meant for a door, and a door alone cannot see that an arc is missing from
    its index. Reading door + arcs from the index also threads ONE identity ledger through the
    whole folder, so a `D3` in two different arcs is caught -- which is invisible file by file.

    `fold_plan` is the single enumeration of a plan's arcs: `read_plan_tree` (used by `make plans`
    and `make ui-json`) and `verify_readability` (used by `make plan-nouveau`) already go through it.
    Rebuilding that walk inline would create a third, divergent enumeration and bypass per-arc
    refusals such as `bloc_non_ferme` and `arc_vide`.

    What the barrier still owns is exactly what only the INDEX can answer, and nothing else: which
    paths exist (`tree`), what MODE each carries (`read_indexed`), and what its bytes are.
    """
    door_path = next(
        (
            (folder + "/" + name if folder else name)
            for name in DOOR_FILENAMES
            if (folder + "/" + name if folder else name) in tree
        ),
        None,
    )
    if door_path is None:
        return folder or ".", [
            "ligne -   : aucune porte (`%s`) dans `%s` -- un arc sans porte n'appartient a "
            "aucun plan" % ("` / `".join(DOOR_FILENAMES), folder or ".")
        ]
    door_text, why = read_indexed(door_path)
    if door_text is None:
        return door_path, ["ligne -   : " + why]

    prefix = (folder + "/") if folder else ""
    under = prefix + ARCS_DIRNAME + "/"
    # `folder_form`: does a `plan/` directory exist at all in the INDEX? A plan that is one file
    # has no index to verify, and reading its prose as index entries refused a real commit once.
    #
    # ... OR did one exist BEFORE this commit? Without the second half, erasing the LAST arc of a
    # plan emptied `<folder>/plan/` from the index, `folder_form` fell back to False, and
    # `index_sans_fichier` -- the one refusal that catches a door still citing a file that no
    # longer exists -- was suppressed by the clause written for single-file plans. The hole this
    # whole pass closes would have reopened on its own worst case, in silence.
    #
    # WARNING:WARNING: THE ONE STATE WHERE THE FOUR SURFACES CANNOT BE MADE TO AGREE, AND IT IS STRUCTURAL.
    # GIT DOES NOT TRACK EMPTY DIRECTORIES. So a plan whose `<folder>/plan/` EXISTS ON THE DISK and
    # holds nothing is, at the same instant:
    #   * FOLDER form for `plan_registry`, which asks the disk -- `(folder / ARCS_DIRNAME).is_dir()`
    #     is True, `index_sans_fichier` is evaluated, and every arc the door names is missing:
    #     `make plans` says ILLISIBLE;
    #   * FILE form here, which asks the index -- no path can start with `<folder>/plan/`, no
    #     erasure was staged, so `folder_form` is False, `index_sans_fichier` is SUPPRESSED by the
    #     clause written for single-file plans, and this barrier exits 0.
    # The two predicates are not two implementations of one question; they are two questions that
    # git guarantees will differ on exactly this population. Neither side can close it: reading the
    # disk here would make the barrier bless bytes nobody is committing (the "TRAP AVOIDED" above),
    # and the registry cannot see an index it does not read.
    # POPULATION: a `plan/` directory that exists and is EMPTY -- which no commit can ever create
    # or preserve, so it only ever exists between two gestures of a person's editor. Every other
    # state (`plan/` absent, `plan/` holding at least one file, `plan/` emptied BY this commit --
    # the `deleted` clause right below) has the four surfaces agreeing.
    # DECLARED rather than closed, and FROZEN by
    # `test_an_EMPTY_plan_directory_is_the_ONE_state_where_the_two_sides_DIVERGE`, which runs both
    # sides on this exact case and asserts the divergence. The day git tracks empty directories,
    # that test goes red -- which is the only notice anyone will get.
    reading = fold_plan(
        door_text,
        # `tree` for the fold is every path under `plan/` RELATIVE TO THE DOOR (`plan/socle.md`,
        # `plan/t1/PLAN.md`). Which of those are THIS plan's arcs is `plan_entries`' answer, inside
        # the fold, and nobody else's -- re-sorting arcs from nested doors out here would be the
        # second enumeration coming back through the window.
        tuple(p[len(prefix) :] for p in tree if p.startswith(under)),
        lambda member: read_indexed(prefix + member),
        folder_form=any(p.startswith(under) for p in tree)
        or any(p.startswith(under) for p in deleted),
        on_loss=lost,
    )
    if is_readable(reading, role=ROLE_PLAN):
        return door_path, []
    lines = ["%s : %s -- %s" % (p.where, p.kind, p.detail) for p in reading.problems]
    lines += ["ligne -   : " + reason for reason in unreadable_reasons(reading, role=ROLE_PLAN)]
    return door_path, lines


paths = [p for p in os.environ.get("MNEMO_STAGED_PLANS", "").split("\n") if p]
#: Every staged path, plan-shaped or not -- the un-filtered list `paths` was carved out of.
staged = tuple(p for p in os.environ.get("MNEMO_STAGED_PATHS", "").split("\n") if p)
tree, MODES = indexed_tree()
#: ERASED = "staged, and ABSENT from the RESULTING index". A question about STATE, asked of
#: `git ls-files` -- which is already read for `tree`, so this costs nothing and replaces a second
#: `git diff --cached --diff-filter=D`. That filter was the same status reasoning one level down,
#: and status reasoning is what let `git mv` through: `D` covered `git rm` and nothing covered a
#: rename git decided to call `R`. Here `git rm`, the source of a `git mv`, an `R` git paired on
#: its own, a `T` that dropped the blob -- and the status nobody has written yet -- all answer the
#: same way, because none of them is asked. Only "is it still there?" is.
present = set(tree)
deleted = tuple(p for p in staged if p not in present)
failures = []
seen_folders = []
for path in paths:
    folder = owning_plan(path, tree)
    if folder is None and path in deleted:
        # ERASED, and no door in the index claims it -- so the door itself is going away. Two very
        # different commits land here and only ONE of them lies: dropping a whole plan (door and
        # arcs together) leaves nothing behind, while dropping the door alone strands every arc.
        # `judge` answers, rather than a sentence written a second time here: with the door gone
        # from the index it takes its own `door_path is None` branch, which is exactly the refusal
        # this case deserves and keeps ONE wording for it.
        if path.rsplit("/", 1)[-1] in DOOR_FILENAMES and arcs_left_behind(path, tree):
            orphaned = folder_of(path)
            if orphaned not in seen_folders:
                seen_folders.append(orphaned)
                failures.append((path, judge(orphaned, tree, deleted)[1]))
        continue
    if folder is None:
        # The staged file matched a plan-shaped pattern and no door claims it. Named rather than
        # skipped: silently ignoring it is precisely the hole this clause was written to close.
        failures.append((path, ["ligne -   : aucune porte au-dessus de ce fichier -- il "
                                "n'appartient a aucun plan et n'est juge par rien"]))
        continue
    if folder in seen_folders:
        continue
    seen_folders.append(folder)
    label, lines = judge(folder, tree, deleted)
    if lines:
        failures.append((label, lines))

if not failures:
    sys.exit(0)

sys.stderr.write("[pre-commit] plan illisible mis en scene -- commit refuse.\n\n")
for path, lines in failures:
    sys.stderr.write("  %s -- etat : %s\n" % (path, ETAT_ILLISIBLE))
    for line in lines:
        sys.stderr.write("    " + line + "\n")
    sys.stderr.write("\n")
sys.stderr.write("Urgence (a n'utiliser qu'en cas de reel besoin) : git commit --no-verify\n")
sys.exit(1)
PYEOF
